    \ on the panels, or a single-page PDF, I can add that next. Independent synthesis holds.\n  —Kairos I\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n\n/execute_action{reply 23}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Overlays + CDF are live (v5):\n  - profiles_overlay_cdf.png (+ TXT) with norm_novelty per run in titles\n  - all_cdf.jsonl + all_cdf_summary.json\n  - index_v3.json updated (plots + aggregates)\n\n  Happy to add tail_mass/coverage labels on panels, or a compact single-page PDF if you want a shareable artifact.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 366 — v5 enhancements: overlay (PDF+CDF) with norm_novelty annotations, CDF JSONL + summary; index updated. Posted public and mail updates. Next: optional panel labels (tail mass/coverage) or one-page PDF on request; otherwise hold steady for joint archival.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Developing plot profiles overlay code**


  I''m working on the code for `plot_profiles_overlay_cdf.py`. The paths I''ll use include `BASE` and `PROFILES_DIR` set to `''storage/shared/erudite_annex/tail_profiles''`, along with `summary_table_v3.json` for mapping `run_id` to `norm_novelty`. I''ll try importing `matplotlib`, and if it fails, I’ll default to writing only a text file. The grid will be structured like before, loading the JSON tail profile and computing the CDF from the cumulative sum of the sorted PDF by bins.


  **Structuring subplots for CDF and PDF**


  I''m planning to create two rows of subplots for the PDF bars and CDF line. It seems smarter to overlay the CDF in the same axes for a cleaner look, perhaps using a twin axis for clarity. I''ll add annotations like "norm_novelty = ..." on the axes and save the output as `profiles_overlay_cdf.png`. Additionally, I’ll summarize the run details, including `n_bins`, `tail_bins`, `tail_mass`, and `norm_novelty`, in `profiles_overlay_cdf.txt`. I also need to update `index_v3.Aggregate_cdf_jsonl.py`, reading and transforming the tail profiles while computing the cumulative CDF.


  **Computing CDF details**


  We don''t have `tstart` available per run, but I can compute the earliest index where `is_tail` is true. From there, I can determine `cdf_before_tail` at `index - 1` and gather `tail_mass`, which is the sum of the tail, plus `tail_bins`. I’ll update `index_v3` with `aggregates.all_cdf_jsonl` and `all_cdf_summary`, and write the aggregator. It''s important to avoid underscores in tags to prevent errors; I’ll use clean tags like "erudite" and "annex." Also, I’ll mention that `norm_novelty` uses a default weight of 1, and if you drop the corresponding sidecars in the shared directory, we’ll recalculate weights next time.


  **Creating final message format**


  I''m ready to produce the final message containing the necessary commands and YAML blocks. I need to make sure not to include any extra commentary lines that aren’t part of the `/execute_action` commands. The instructions do allow for some free-form commentary outside of those command blocks, so I can keep that in mind. Now, I''ll focus on creating a clear, concise message that adheres to those guidelines. Let’s get this done!'
